WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:03.470
Every nation that's a coastal state

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.590
has some region of the seafloor that falls

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:09.040
within their territorial
waters and many countries,

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.030
including the U.S. the
area that's underwater

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.963
exceeds the area that's above water.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:20.100
So huge amounts of nation's areas

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.890
and regions are just really
unexplored, uncharacterized.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.590
We don't really know what's there.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.693
We know less about our seabed

00:00:28.693 --> 00:00:32.030
than we do about the
surface of the Moon or Mars.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:35.040
Only 20% of it has been mapped.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.410
A map of the seafloor
could lead to all kinds

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:40.120
of discoveries, like new marine species

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.090
and rare earth minerals, like cobalt

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.693
that we need for electric
cars and mobile phones.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.740
The value of all the gold
deposits alone on the seafloor

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:52.146
is estimated to be around $150 trillion.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.840
A map could also help
us when tragedy strikes.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.840
Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 disappeared

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.040
from radar screens.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.360
The mapping of the ocean
floor is being carried out.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.610
The problem is that the
Southern Indian Ocean

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.150
is just so incredibly huge.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:11.420
To me a map is a fundamental
piece of understanding

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.250
of who we are, where we
are, where things are.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.050
Once you understand something,
you start to value it more

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.680
and when you start to value something,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.460
that's when you start
to really think about

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.440
making it healthy and preserving
it and keeping it going.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.330
A map is the first step,

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:28.010
it's the basic understanding that you need

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:28.896
to figure out where you are.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:39.840
Oceans,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.500
they cover over 70% of the planet

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.913
but most of what lies
underneath is a mystery.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.730
The seafloor is a really
fascinating landscape.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.070
We don't have a lot of data

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.550
to describe the shape of it in detail

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:58.050
but in places where we do
there are spectacular features.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.130
There's meandering channels

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.020
with all kinds of interesting topography.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.780
There are canyons, there are
seamounts, mid-ocean ridges,

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.760
all kinds of things that we see on land

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.610
that are just basically
hidden from us by the water.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.090
As it stands, this is what a
map of the seabed looks like.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:19.620
All that black, those are the
parts we haven't mapped yet.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.210
At present most people think

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.830
that the seafloor is actually mapped

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.250
because you'll see a world ocean map,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.850
and you can see some
shape of the seafloor.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.810
But most of that is based on prediction.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:35.050
Roughly 80% of what you see
for the world ocean floor

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:39.110
is modeled based on measurements
of the sea surface height

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.253
and a computation based on gravity.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.560
In 2017, The Nippon Foundation

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.070
and the General Bathymetric
Chart of the Oceans or GEBCO

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:50.703
launched Seabed 2030.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.760
It's a collaborative project to collect

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.670
and collate data oceanwide

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.660
and create a complete map of the seafloor

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:58.910
by the end of the decade.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.690
To do this would take
one ship around 350 years

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.200
at a cost of up to $3 billion.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.180
That's why data is harvested
by all kinds of vessels

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.450
from fishing boats to freighters.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:14.090
Because there is so little data

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.940
it's important that
vessels that are capable

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.070
and willing to acquire data
when they're just moving

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:22.550
around the ocean, do so.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:26.290
So we have a lot of data that's
collected during transits.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.970
There's a lot of academic requirements

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:30.880
to make data publicly available.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.130
There's industry data that's
starting to become more

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:35.720
and more available,
there's government data.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.530
So there's a lot of different ways

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.770
that this data is coming
in, and it's a really messy

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.830
and challenging puzzle to put together.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.590
But what's exciting is that
it's a puzzle to put together.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:49.800
Since Seabed 2030 launched,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.380
the area mapped has risen to 20%

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.600
and all the information
collected is freely available.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.940
The more data we have collectively

00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.840
and other people can access,

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.900
the faster the discoveries are made.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.270
Which really pushes
forward our understanding

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:08.630
at a faster rate than if
you held onto that data

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.150
and it was only you with that data.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.340
Things like machine learning

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:15.070
and artificial intelligence
are also going to kick in

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:18.280
in the next decade,
because with all this data

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.750
they'll be able to make
connections a lot faster

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.530
than perhaps we would have been able to.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.980
Which then again, feeds
our human understanding

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.160
and also gives us new ideas

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:32.083
and new ways of thinking, of exploration.

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.070
Until the early 20th century

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.050
mapping technology was simple.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:40.150
Usually a weight attached to a long rope

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:41.820
known as a lead line.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.260
After the Titanic sank in 1912,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.180
explorers proposed using
sound waves to find its hull

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.660
in the North Atlantic.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:49.970
This led to sonar,

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.420
the most common technique
used for mapping today.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.410
Operated by the Schmidt Ocean Institute,

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.960
the Falkor is an 82 meter
long research vessel

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.800
equipped with two multibeam sonar systems

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.170
that have helped Deborah Smith

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.240
and a team of other marine technicians

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.710
to map over a million square
kilometers of the seafloor.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.320
You know, you go from somebody

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:16.320
with a rope with some knots
to sonar acoustics that is,

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.750
I call it math magic,

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.530
but it is incredibly advanced
mathematical technology

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.930
to be able to measure the distance

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.973
and time of travel of
sound on a moving ship.

00:05:30.023 --> 00:05:33.260
Multibeam mapping works by using sonars.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:36.920
So sonar is taking sound
and the measurement of time.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:38.860
So you have a multibeam sonar

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.840
and it sends out a ping of sound.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.800
That sound travels
through the water column,

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.840
hits the seafloor and
comes back to the ship

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.460
and we measure that time

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.680
and we're able to calculate the distance.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:52.270
Each individual ping of
sound has many, many beams.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.210
So it looks like a giant
fan across the seafloor

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.630
and that fan is moving along the seafloor.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:00.350
So you have many tiny,
little beams going across

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.810
and they're traveling along a track.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:05.870
You can look at that data
and look at both the peaks

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:07.480
and the valleys and the highs and the lows

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:10.225
and sort of create a
three-dimensional image

00:06:10.225 --> 00:06:11.593
of the seafloor.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:16.470
The accuracy of multibeam
sonar really depends

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.160
on the water depth that you're in.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.270
Think of it like a flashlight beam

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:21.650
and if you're holding a flashlight

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:23.280
and you're shining it on the floor,

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.440
the closer you get to the
floor with your flashlight,

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.210
the smaller that beam is.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:30.260
If you have 400 of these beams all across,

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.640
so think of 400 people
holding a flashlight.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.090
The closer they hold that
flashlight to the seafloor,

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:37.670
the smaller your beam is
which also means the smaller

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.250
that you can find
information or data about.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:44.840
So it really depends on the
depth of the seafloor in order

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:47.743
to see how much you can define an object.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.670
This variation in depth
means that mapping resolution

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:55.210
will range from 100 x 100
meters in shallow waters

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:59.050
to 800 x 800 meters in the
deepest parts of the ocean,

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:01.863
which go down as far as 11,000 meters.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:08.070
Remotely operated vehicles
or ROV's can then be sent down

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.720
to investigate seabed features up close.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.080
We've just crossed the 1,000 meter mark.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.990
It's 4.6 degrees C and now
we're gonna drive the ROV down

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:20.870
at a high rate towards the seafloor.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:22.510
On a recent mapping expedition,

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:24.150
scientists on the Falkor discovered

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:26.720
a 500-meter-tall coral structure

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.800
in Australia's Great Barrier Reef.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.643
The first to be discovered
in over 120 years.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:35.760
A place that is incredibly
popular, well visited,

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.280
well-traveled, you know, we're
still finding new things.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.720
You don't know until you map it.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:42.500
And so until you get that sort
of high-resolution picture

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:43.910
you don't know the sand waves

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:45.900
or the old riverbeds that used to be there

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:47.900
or the waterfalls off
the side of a volcano

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.410
that used to be above the surface

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:50.800
and now is below the surface.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:52.330
It's a little bit of everything.

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:54.940
Think of, let's say Hawaii or someplace

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.780
where you see a volcano above the water.

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:58.253
Picture that below the water.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:01.780
I'm really kind of obsessed

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:04.150
with the whole concept of scale here.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.860
Although we can create
3D models and, you know,

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.250
kind of come up with these visualizations.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:11.280
It's not the same as that feeling

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.720
of looking out over this big landscape.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:17.020
But I mean, I think we'll get there soon

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:21.000
with VR and other kinds of
technology, but it's really

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:23.890
spectacular to think about,

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:26.060
especially with the familiarity
that some of us have

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:28.440
with these features,
like what it looks like.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.320
We look out at the ocean
and it just looks flat

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:32.120
maybe with little bumps.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.570
We really don't think about
all of this that's underneath

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:38.050
and it's really an amazing landscape

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:39.400
that's just hidden from us.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:50.150
With more than 90% of the
ocean deeper than 200 meters,

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:51.670
fleets of autonomous vehicles

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:54.270
could make the data
gathering process faster,

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.400
cheaper, and safer

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:57.780
and new discoveries might help

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.890
with exploration of all kinds.

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.840
I think the ocean can actually provide us

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:06.060
with huge insights as we
go out into space as well

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.010
and look for life and
other kinds of life forms.

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:12.630
I also think in this next few years

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:16.050
we will have a different
kind of access to the ocean

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:19.460
because of virtual reality
and augmented reality.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:23.150
And so that will allow us
to sit in our living rooms

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:26.480
and really experience what's
under water in a different way

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:28.533
and see this different alien planet.

